nd joycelyn the uk and the eu class  biomass as renewable and in fact  just to remind us the government does  say that biomass is a key element of  plans to to reach net zero targets what  are your thoughts on this  i mean i think i'd have to second what  bruce has said we need to really  reconsider what we mean by sustainable  and as the science has proved this isn't  really sustainable um when trees are  such an important part of our strategy  to get to net zero and i'm not a biofuel  expert but i think another important  thing we need to keep in mind is that  um kind of at the same time as a climate  crisis we have a biodiversity crisis and  planting trees just to be cut down again  does nothing for our biodiversity  nothing for natural ecosystems which are  equally as important and we can't just  keep focusing on this net zero kind of  calculation down to zero because it's  not a true  um kind of signifier of our progress  bruce you you don't think the biomass is  should be classed as a renewable source  of energy what  what technology might help you change  your mind if there was the technology to  capture the carbon released when the  wood is burnt  would that make a big difference and how  far away are we from that  so as jocelyn said we're trying to solve  several crises at the same time one is  climate and the other is the ecological  crisis  um we need trees for both both to pull  carbon out of the atmosphere and also to  make sure there is homes for  a lot of wildlife that's teetering on  the edge of extinction so again this  this makes no sense and um the the path  forward is how do we phase out biomass  now that the uk is very close to being  done with coal  the path ahead is investing in offshore  wind and solar and all the things that  are allowing the country to make great  progress on coal now to make the same  progress on phasing out biomass  you
 

